   "_dt": 1794849594.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "Mr. Modi will participate in the 23rd ASEAN-India Summit and the 21st East Asia Summit on Tuesday (November 17). Marcos Jr. “At the ASEAN-India Summit, I look forward to working with fellow leaders to build on the progress of our Comprehensive Strategic Partnership and give further momentum to our shared aspirations for growth, connectivity and prosperity,” he added.",
   "summary_zh": "Modi先生将于周二（11月17日）参加第23届东盟—印度峰会和第21届东亚峰会。Marcos Jr.补充道：“在东盟—印度峰会上，我期待与其他领导人合作，在我们的全面战略伙伴关系所取得的进展基础上继续努力，进一步推动我们关于增长、互联互通和繁荣的共同愿望。”",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Monday (November 16, 2026) arrived in the Philippines to attend two key summits, aiming to strengthen ties with Southeast Asian and East Asian nations and to discuss regional and global challenges. Mr. Modi will participate in the 23rd ASEAN-India Summit and the 21st East Asia Summit on Tuesday (November 17). He is there at the invitation of Philippine President Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. “At the ASEAN-India Summit, I look forward to working with fellow leaders to build on the progress of our Comprehensive Strategic Partnership and give further momentum to our shared aspirations for growth, connectivity and prosperity,” he added. Talking about The East Asia Summit, Mr. Modi said that it ‘provides an important platform for dialogue on regional and global challenges’. “I look forward to exchanging views with participating leaders on strengthening peace, stability and cooperation in the Indo-Pacific,” the Prime Minister said. Mr. Modi also said he looks forward to interacting with the vibrant Indian community in the Philippines ‘whose contributions strengthen the bonds of friendship between our two countries’. “I am confident that the visit will impart new energy to India’s partnership with ASEAN and reinforce our collective commitment to a peaceful, prosperous and resilient Indo-Pacific,” he added. A meeting of the leaders of the Quad group — comprising the U.S., India, Australia and Japan — is scheduled to be held in Manila on the sidelines of the ASEAN Summit, a senior U.S. official said. The meeting of the Quad leaders, the first in two years, will bring together Prime Minister Narendra Modi, U.S. President Donald Trump, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese, and Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi. The last meeting of Quad leaders was chaired by then U.S. President Joe Biden in Delaware on September 21, 2024. “On Tuesday (November 17, 2026) evening in Manila… the President will participate in a multilateral meeting with Quad leaders to discuss shared interests of peace, prosperity, and security in the Indo-Pacific region,” Olivia Wales, Assistant Press Secretary at the White House, said. She said under President Trump’s leadership, the Quad has demonstrated that it is a partnership of action, tackling the region’s toughest challenges and driving opportunities to ensure a free and open Indo-Pacific. The Quad group was created in 2007, but went mostly dormant the year after amid concerns about provoking China. In 2017, Mr. Trump revived it to address growing concerns about China’s increasing economic, diplomatic and military heft in the Indo-Pacific, though he never held a leaders’ summit. The Manila meeting will be the Quad leaders’ first meeting during Mr. Trump’s present term as U.S. President. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
