   "title": "India and Egypt form working group to explore trade deal contours",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/piyush-goyal-in-egypt-for-india-egypt-joint-trade-committee-jtc-meet/article71706845.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:46:42 +0530",
   "_dt": 1794849402.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription India and Egypt have discussed ways to reduce non-tariff barriers, facilitate customs processes, strengthen standards and improve market access with an aim to nearly double bilateral trade to $12 billion, an official statement said on Monday (November 16, 2026).",
   "summary_zh": "解锁这些内容需订阅。印度和埃及已讨论减少非关税壁垒、便利海关流程、强化标准和改善市场准入的方法，目标是将双边贸易额 Nearly double 至120亿美元。一名官员周一（2026年11月16日）在一份官方声明中表示了上述情况。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription India and Egypt have discussed ways to reduce non-tariff barriers, facilitate customs processes, strengthen standards and improve market access with an aim to nearly double bilateral trade to $12 billion, an official statement said on Monday (November 16, 2026). The two countries have decided to set up a Joint Working Group to explore the Terms of Reference for a Preferential Trade Agreement (PTA) or Free Trade Agreement (FTA) to facilitate stronger bilateral trade, improve access to both markets and promote investment linkages between the two countries. India, U.S. agreed on preferential market access for Indian goods in America: Goyal Besides, the two countries deliberated on resolving issues affecting business operations, including product registrations and trade-related regulatory procedures. These issues figured during the India-Egypt Joint Trade Committee (JTC) meet, which was co-chaired by Commerce and Industry Minister Piyush Goyal and Mohamed Farid Saleh, Minister of Investment and Foreign Trade of Egypt, in Cairo on Sunday (November 15, 2026). The committee provided an opportunity to review existing trade ties, assess challenges faced by businesses and identify new areas for closer collaboration. \"Both sides reaffirmed their commitment to expanding bilateral trade, with a target of $12 billion. They emphasised the need to diversify trade, promote value-added exports and address trade-related challenges,\" the Commerce Ministry said. Currently, the bilateral trade is at $6.5 billion and both sides are targeting to double it in the coming years. The discussions also focused on strengthening business exchanges and encouraging joint manufacturing and investment, it said. Priority sectors included pharmaceuticals and medical devices, engineering and electrical equipment, fertilisers, textiles, renewable energy and emerging technologies. Opportunities for Indian investment in Egyptian industrial zones, as well as Egyptian investment in India's expanding manufacturing and services sectors, also formed an important part of the meeting, it added. Both sides expressed interest in strengthening the framework agreement between Invest India and Egypt's General Authority for Investment and Free Zones, including a proposed digital platform for sharing investment information and facilitating business linkages. Egypt's proximity to the Suez Canal and its connectivity with African, Arab and European markets makes it a valuable partner for export-oriented manufacturing and regional supply chains. Addressing a session, Mr. Goyal proposed a four-point action agenda ahead of the 80th anniversary of India-Egypt diplomatic relations in 2027. The Ministry said Egypt's strategic location linking the Mediterranean and Red Seas, its expanding ports and logistics infrastructure, and access to African, Arab and European markets offer opportunities for Indian businesses seeking to establish manufacturing and export-oriented operations. The next JTC meeting was agreed to be held in New Delhi in 2028, or earlier at a mutually convenient date. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
