   "summarySource": "index",
   "body": "The 2026 economic outturn supports the government’s claim that stabilisation has advanced. Real GDP grew 6.2% in the first half of 2026; headline inflation was 5.5% in October. During January–October, the commitment-basis primary surplus reached 1.4% of GDP, and the cash deficit was 2.3% of GDP against a programmed 4.5%. Public debt stood at 47.5% of GDP at end-October. Average commercial-bank lending rates had fallen to about 15.9%, while private-sector credit was expanding at 34% year on year in September. These developments suggest that Ghana’s macroeconomic conditions are generally moving in a favorable direction, providing a stronger foundation for sustained economic stability and growth",
