   "_dt": 1794825049.0,
   "ageDays": 0.32,
   "summary": "“If we want to influence the South China Sea in 2031, we must recognize that strategic competition has changed,” Oban said in his opening remarks at the Manila Dialogue on the South China Sea. His remarks came as China continued to exert maritime pressure in the South China Sea, a strategic waterway through which trillions of dollars in trade pass annually and where Philippine and Chinese vessels have been involved in recurring collisions and confrontations.",
   "summary_zh": "“如果我们希望在2031年影响南海局势，就必须认识到战略竞争已经发生变化，”奥班在马尼拉南海对话会的开幕致辞中说道。他发表这番言论之际，中国继续在南海施加海上压力。这条战略水道每年承载数万亿美元贸易额，菲律宾与中国船只曾多次发生碰撞和对抗。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "“If we want to influence the South China Sea in 2031, we must recognize that strategic competition has changed,” Oban said in his opening remarks at the Manila Dialogue on the South China Sea. His remarks came as China continued to exert maritime pressure in the South China Sea, a strategic waterway through which trillions of dollars in trade pass annually and where Philippine and Chinese vessels have been involved in recurring collisions and confrontations. Beijing’s claim is based on a nine-dash line map dating to the 1940s that encompasses the Philippines’ exclusive economic zone (EEZ), part of which Manila calls the West Philippine Sea. A United Nations-backed tribunal invalidated China’s claim in 2016, ruling that it had no legal basis under international law. “Strategic competition must never make coercion an accepted means of managing differences,” he said. “Preventing armed conflict remains essential, but preventing the gradual erosion of regional maritime order is primordial.” And he noted that a five-year horizon through 2031 will prove decisive in determining whether international rules prevail over “coercion and unilateral actions” in the South China Sea. “Several developments will shape this period, including China’s continuing military modernization, the Philippines’ next national security planning cycle, and Asean’s implementation of regional economic and security priorities,” Oban said. READ: Ridon: We respect ties with China, but West Philippine Sea is ours For the Philippines, he said, the path to 2031 requires forging deeper security alliances without sacrificing diplomatic boundaries, while bolstering regional mechanisms for maritime safety, including that Asean and its partners must translate commitments into practical arrangements to prevent incidents at sea. Building greater national resilience by fortifying the country’s physical and digital infrastructure to reduce systemic vulnerabilities should also be a priority, he added. Your subscription could not be saved. Please try again. Your subscription has been successful. “By 2031, our success will not be about acquired capabilities, exercises conducted, or agreements signed. It will be defined by safe maritime locations, more secure local rights, resilient critical infrastructures, and unhampered freedom of action all within the ambit of international law,” Oban said. /dl For comprehensive coverage, in-depth analysis, visit our special page for West Philippine Sea updates . Stay informed with articles, videos, and expert opinions",
