   "title": "ASEAN, China won’t meet 2026 deadline to finish South China Sea talks",
   "url": "https://www.rappler.com/philippines/asean-china-fail-meet-deadline-sea-code-conduct-talks-2026/",
   "domain": "rappler.com",
   "source": "rappler.com",
   "published": "Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:55:00 +0800",
   "_dt": 1794794100.0,
   "ageDays": 0.68,
   "summary": "MANILA, Philippines – The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) and China will not meet their deadline to conclude negotiations for the long-delayed Code of Conduct (COC) in the South China Sea under the chairmanship of the Philippines in 2026. The proposed COC is aimed at setting rules for ASEAN and China to follow to reduce tensions in the South China Sea. In 2023, ASEAN foreign ministers set 2026 as the deadline for the conclusion of talks on the proposed COC.",
   "summary_zh": "菲律宾马尼拉——在菲律宾担任2026年主席国期间，东南亚国家联盟（ASEAN）和中国将无法按期完成南海行为准则（COC）谈判。这项拖延已久的谈判旨在为ASEAN和中国制定应遵守的规则，以缓解南海紧张局势。2023年，ASEAN各国外交部长将2026年定为完成拟议中的COC谈判的期限。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. RETREAT. Leaders gather for the 49th ASEAN Summit retreat session at the Coconut Palace in Pasay City on November 15, 2026. MANILA, Philippines – The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) and China will not meet their deadline to conclude negotiations for the long-delayed Code of Conduct (COC) in the South China Sea under the chairmanship of the Philippines in 2026. On Sunday, November 15, Philippine Foreign Secretary Maria Theresa Lazaro said ASEAN and China have reached provisional agreement on over 80% of the draft text. “The negotiations now enter their final phase and we commend the dedication and constructive engagement of all delegations,” Lazaro said in a briefing on the opening day of the 49th ASEAN Summit and Related Summits . “The parties agree to continue to work constructively with a view to concluding the negotiations on an effective and substantive Code of Conduct at the earliest possible time,” she said, reading from the detailed briefing statement . The proposed COC is aimed at setting rules for ASEAN and China to follow to reduce tensions in the South China Sea. China and several ASEAN member states, including the Philippines, have overlapping claims in the resource-rich waterway. The pending milestone issues are whether the COC will be legally binding, its geographic scope, and how terms would be defined in the code. A provisional agreement was reached regarding the relationship between the non-binding 2002 Declaration on the Conduct of Parties in the South China Sea (DOC) and the proposed COC. Lazaro said the parties affirmed that the existing DOC will provide the foundation for the proposed COC. The COC will also set out “more effective and substantive provisions” to guide conduct in the South China Sea. In addition, they agreed that “the COC is intended to serve as a mechanism for the constructive management of differences at sea, and not to settle disputes,” according to Lazaro. In 2023, ASEAN foreign ministers set 2026 as the deadline for the conclusion of talks on the proposed COC. With negotiations on crucial issues still not completed, Lazaro sought to explain some of the “sensitivities,” such as potential ratification on the part of certain ASEAN member states in order to make the proposed document legally binding. “I think there was not enough time. If we were given probably another five to six months, palagay ko (I think) we can finish that,” Lazaro said of the talks. As ASEAN chair in 2026, the Philippines initiated nine rounds of negotiations and two senior officials’ meetings for the COC. Lazaro said the proposed text was only at 50% before the country’s shepherding. “Konti na lang pag-uusapan nila sa Singapore (They have few details left to talk about in Singapore),” she also said, adding that she trusts Singapore will be able to finish the discussions. Singapore will assume the ASEAN chairmanship in 2027. – Rappler.com Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ph",
    "vn"
   ],
